WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.362
Why does routing matter?

2
00:00:01.362 --> 00:00:02.500
Three reasons.

3
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:06.000
First, a notice carries
no owner and no due date.

4
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.817
Routing is where each notice
that needs action gets both:

5
00:00:09.817 --> 00:00:13.877
a typed task in the front door,
not a second queue, with an owner,

6
00:00:13.877 --> 00:00:17.613
a severity, a response window,
and the provider's own date.

7
00:00:17.613 --> 00:00:20.276
Second, providers repeat themselves.

8
00:00:20.276 --> 00:00:23.450
The same notice comes back
in every weekly read.

9
00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:26.475
A router that ticketed it every
week would fill the queue,

10
00:00:26.475 --> 00:00:28.213
and people would stop reading it.

11
00:00:28.213 --> 00:00:30.479
So the router remembers
what it has routed,

12
00:00:30.479 --> 00:00:33.199
and writes nothing for a
notice that hasn't changed.

13
00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:37.862
Third, a provider closing its notice
isn't the agency's work being done.

14
00:00:37.862 --> 00:00:40.613
A retirement can close
on the provider's side

15
00:00:40.613 --> 00:00:43.587
while the agency still
runs the retired version.

16
00:00:43.587 --> 00:00:49.076
So a task closes only when the agency's own
next collection shows the notice closed,

17
00:00:49.076 --> 00:00:52.062
or the agency's resources
no longer affected.

18
00:00:52.062 --> 00:00:53.300
That's the re-read.
